narrative:
## Functions That Work with Structs

Regular functions (not methods) can take structs as parameters and return structs as results. This separates data creation from data behavior.

### Factory Functions

A function that creates and returns a struct is called a *factory*:

```xiom
type Person = {
  name: Str;
  age: Int;
}

fn create_person(name: Str, age: Int) -> Person {
  return Person{ name: name, age: age };
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let p1 = create_person("Dana", 17);
  let p2 = create_person("Eli", 13);
  io.println(p1.name);
  io.println(p2.name);
  return 0;
}
```

### Functions That Take Structs as Input

```xiom
fn print_person(person: Person) {
  io.println(person.name + " is " + " years old.");
  io.println(person.age);
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let p = create_person("Frankie", 15);
  print_person(p);
  return 0;
}
```

### Return Types Are Structs Too

You've returned `Int`, `Str`, and `Bool` — now you can return a whole struct:

```xiom
fn oldest(a: Person, b: Person) -> Person {
  if a.age > b.age {
    return a;
  }
  return b;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let young = Person{ name: "Grace", age: 11 };
  let old = Person{ name: "Henry", age: 18 };
  let winner = oldest(young, old);
  io.println(winner.name);  // Henry
  return 0;
}
```

Functions and structs work together naturally — functions do the heavy lifting, structs carry the data.

